  {
    "id": "mapgpt",
    "title": "MapGPT: Map-Guided Prompting with Adaptive Path Planning for Vision-and-Language Navigation",
    "authors": [
      "Jiaqi Chen",
      "Bingqian Lin",
      "Ran Xu",
      "Zhenhua Chai",
      "Xiaodan Liang",
      "Kwan-Yee K. Wong"
    ],
    "year": 2024,
    "venue": "ACL 2024 long papers",
    "source": "https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.529/",
    "pages": "1, 3–7",
    "problem": "Locally choosing a neighboring viewpoint leaves zero-shot LLM agents unable to reason about unexplored branches and backtracking. Discrete MatterSim provides connectivity and navigable candidates; this is not learned free-space discovery or continuous locomotion. REVERIE evaluation covers navigation but explicitly omits object grounding.",
    "method": "§3 Eq.1 generates thought T_t and categorical action from task description, instruction, action history, observations and candidates. Eq.2 adds online map M_t and previous plan P_(t−1), then produces T_t,P_t,a_t. G_t=(V_t,E_t) stores observed node IDs and simulator-provided edges; textual prompts distinguish visited, currently accessible and observed-but-inaccessible nodes. Their annotations are candidate images (GPT4V) or BLIP2/FasterRCNN descriptions (GPT4). Current/visited annotations are reused from action/history; other frontier descriptions go into supplementary context. The plan is updated each step and may include multiple contingencies, but no search-optimality constraint is imposed. The action is a letter over N neighbors plus STOP. There is no navigation-training objective; pretrained caption/detection and paid GPT inference remain dependencies. REVERIE 'Surroundings' uses dataset object lists for stopping, whereas R2R does not use them (§3.1.2). Map connectivity is the DUET-style graph data structure, not the DUET learned dual-scale policy. Text-only system reports672input/115outputtokens/step vsNavGPT2465/317.",
    "novelty": "An online linguistic topological graph and recurrent editable plan in a single expert prompt. Backtracking and graph memory predate it in learned VLN; contribution adapts their representation to frozen GPT4/4V. Analogical adds joint candidate captions; EvoNav adds retrieved cross-episode experience; HAM-VLN adds graph retrieval/compression and reflective memory.",
    "results": "Table1 216trajectories across72scenes GPT4V47.7SR/38.1SPL; Table2 783instructions11unseen scenes43.7/34.8 vsNavGPT34/29. Text-only38.8SR improves success but25.8SPL is belowNavGPT29. Table3 REVERIE500sample31.6/20.3; trainedHAMT/DUET retested on same subset. These are subset benchmarks, not fullR2R all-instruction leaderboard numbers. §4.2 backtracking in49%episodes and successful correction within80%ofthose are descriptive operational statistics, not causal evidence that every return helps. Extra graph/context/API model differences confound broad comparisons.",
    "bottlenecks": [
      {
        "id": "MAPGPT-1",
        "description": "Exploration gains can lengthen paths and visual-to-text conversion loses discriminative cues",
        "conditions": "Text-only GPT4, several plausible branches",
        "cause": "Descriptions discard visual evidence and repeated planning encourages further exploration",
        "evidence": "Table2 GPT4SR38.8/SPL25.8; §4.2 explanation",
        "followups": "Analogical comparisons, EvoNav visual banks, HAM-VLN selective graph memory; uncertainty of detection and fair cost controls persist",
        "kind": "Methodological / insufficiently tested",
        "status": "Partially addressed; frontier remains uncertain",
        "confidence": "Moderate"
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Training-free map prompting relies on simulator candidates and omits REVERIE grounding",
        "evidence_type": "Author protocol",
        "source": "Supplied PDF",
        "locator": "§3.2, §4.1"
      }
    ],
    "limitations": "Main method/results read; detailed prompts/remaining ablations in appendix not yet reviewed. No benchmark executed."
